{"id": "q1", "target_chain_ids": ["base_c1", "base_c2"], "focus": "rule_conditions", "question": "Does the chart's y-axis 'Value' scale (0-65) represent the same metric as the printed percentage labels, or is it a separate, unexplained scale that makes the geometric reading (Quality highest) incompatible with the label reading (Design highest)?"},
{"id": "q2", "target_chain_ids": ["base_c1", "base_c3"], "focus": "coverage", "question": "Are the public option labels 'Select Design', 'Select Quality', and 'Select Support' the only available choices, meaning that if the true highest category were 'Other', 'Delivery', or 'Price', the form would lack a corresponding option?"},
{"id": "q3", "target_chain_ids": ["base_c2"], "focus": "observations", "question": "Is the vertical position of the Quality point (y≈60) definitively higher than the Support point (y≈54) and Design point (y≈42) on the Value axis, confirming the geometric reading that Quality is the highest point?"}
],
"summary": "I inspected the chart and the initial argument set. The set covers the primary conflict: printed labels (Design 52% highest) vs. geometric position (Quality y≈60 highest). The public task explicitly says 'labeled rating share', which strongly supports the label reading (base_c1, base_c3). The geometric reading (base_c2) is a valid challenge but is likely a distractor or a mislabeled axis. The main gap is whether the y-axis 'Value' is meant to be the same metric as the percentage labels, and whether the form's limited options (Design, Quality, Support) would be a problem if the true answer were a different category. The set does not exhaustively cover the possibility that the y-axis is a different metric, nor does it address the form's option limitations."
}
